of blocks of period p = lcm(|S0 |, . . . , |Ss−1 |). It suffices to define function φ
as: φ(µbp ) = (Bℓ )ℓ∈JpK with Bℓ = {ij,ℓ mod |Sj | | j ∈ JsK}. The other way of
considering µbp is: ∀j ∈ JsK, ∀k ∈ J|Sj |K, ij,k ∈ µ⋆bp (t) ⇐⇒ k = t mod |Sj |.
    A local clocks update mode µlc = (P, ∆), with P = (p0 , . . . , pn−1 ) and
∆ = (δ0 , . . . , δn−1 ), is an update mode such that each automaton i of JnK
is associated with a period pi ∈ N∗ and an initial shift δi ∈ Jpi K such that
i ∈ µ⋆lc (t) ⇐⇒ t = δi mod pi , with t ∈ N.
    Let us now introduce three particular cases or subfamilies of these three
latter update mode families. The parallel update mode µpar = (JnK) makes ev-
ery automaton update its state at each time step, such that ∀t ∈ N, µ⋆par (t) =
JnK. A bipartite update mode µbip = (B0 , B1 ) is a block-sequential update
mode composed of two blocks such that the automata in a same block do
not act on each other (notice that if the grid is finite and the boundary
condition is periodic, then this definition induces that such update modes
are necessarily associated with grids of even size, and that there are exactly
two bipartite update modes, depending on if the even numbered cells are
updated first or second.) A sequential update mode µseq = (ϕ(JnK)), where
ϕ(JnK) = {i0 }, . . . , {in−1 } is a permutation of JnK, makes one and only one
automaton update its state at each time step so that all automata have up-
dated their state after n time steps depending on the order induced by ϕ.
All these update modes follow the order of inclusion pictured in Figure 2.
    As we focus on periodic update modes, let us differentiate two kinds of
time steps. A substep is a time step at which a subset of automata change
their states. A step is the composition of substeps having occurred over a
period.




