Theorem 4. Rules 72 and 76 always reach fixed points.
Proof. We know that x = 0n is a fixed point, so let x = 0a1 1b1 . . . 0ak 1bk ,
since fr (111) = 0 for r ∈ {72, 76}, meaning that the configuration y = 1n
will become f (y) = 0a1 1b1 . . . 0ak 1bk .
Since fr (011) = fr (110) = 1 and fr (001) = fr (100) = 0, for all r ∈ {72, 76},
groups composed of exactly two 1’s are walls, meaning that once the word
0110 appears it will not change.
This also means that groups of ones larger than two cannot increase in size.
In any other case, groups of 1’s of size at least three will decrease in size in
each iteration until:
   • case 1: the group is left with exactly two ones, in which case it becomes
     a wall and remains unchanging.

   • case 2: for rule 72, f72 (010)) = 0, isolated 1’s will disappear, with which
                                                        ′      ′       ′
     the resulting fixed points can be written as 0a1 110a2 . . . 110ak′ 11.

                                       13
    • case 3: for rule 76, f76 (010) = 1, isolated 1’s will be preserved, which
                                                                 ′  ′   ′   ′         ′    ′
      means that the resulting fixed points can be written as 0a1 1b1 0a2 1b2 . . . 0ak′ 1bk′ ,
      with bi ∈ {1, 2}, i ∈ {1, . . . , k ′ }.



