Lemma 28. ECA rule 178 admits two walls, 01 and 10, which are relative
Lemma 28. ECA rule 178 admits two walls, 01 and 10, which are relative
walls.
walls.
Proof. Let a, b ∈ B. Notice that by definition of the rule: f (a00) ̸= f (b00)
and f (00a) ̸= f (00b), and f (a11) ̸= f (b11) and f (11a) ̸= f (11b) if a ̸= b;
f (a01) = 1 and f (01a) = 0; and f (a10) = 0 and ((10a) = 1. Thus, neither
00 nor 01 nor 10 nor 11 are absolute 45
                                      walls.

                                                          47
Proof. Let a, b ∈ B. Notice that by definition of the rule: f (a00) ̸= f (b00)
and f (00a) ̸= f (00b), and f (a11) ̸= f (b11) and f (11a) ̸= f (11b) if a ̸= b;
f (a01) = 1 and f (01a) = 0; and f (a10) = 0 and ((10a) = 1. Thus, neither
00 nor 01 nor 10 nor 11 are absolute walls.
    From what precedes, notice that the properties of 00 and 11 prevent them
to be relative walls. Consider now the two words u = 01 and v = 10 and
let us show that they constitute relative walls. Regardless the states of the
cells surrounding u, every time both cells of u are updated simultaneously,
u changes to v and similarly, v will change to u independently of the states
of the cells that surround it, as long as both its cells are updated together.
Thus, u and v are relative walls.
   We will show certains bp and lc update modes that are able to produce
these relative walls.

Theorem 29. Each representative of (178, bip) applied over a grid of size n
has largest limit cycles of length Θ(n).

Sketch of proof. From lemma 28 we know that the representatives of (178, bip)
do not have walls, because by definition of bip two consecutive cells cannot
be updated simultaneously.
Thus we can start by first considering configurations x composed by one
group of contiguous 1s and proving that if the number of 1s is odd then the
dynamics lead to homogeneous fixed points. Meanwhile, if the number of 1s
is even, then such configurations lead to limit cycles of length n2 .
Secondly, we consider configurations with several groups of contiguous 1s,
which can be analyzed by studying the behaviour with just two groups of
1s. These kinds of configurations are divided into 10 cases, depending on the
parity of the size of the groups, and the local bipartite update mode that
each of the groups follows. We prove that each case converges either to a
homogeneous fixed point or to limit cycles of length n2 .

